## Access

[grantr](https://lattice.alexnodeland.com/grantr/) decides who may do what ([`access.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/access.py)). Each library's `Workspaces` asks it before every command and read, on every surface. A tenant's `owner` and `admin` may do anything in it; a workspace's `admin`, `editor` and `viewer` what their names say; and the application's own principal, which its runner, reactor, mirrors and rules act as, holds the `application` role, granted when it first starts. [`access.md`](access.md) is every role against every action: a test compares it with the libraries' catalogs, so a library's new action, or a change to a role, is reviewed here (`make access-matrix` rewrites it).

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `make operator EMAIL=...` | Make a user the deployment's first operator, who may create tenants |
| `make tenant NAME=... OWNER=...` | Create a tenant, with its owner; once |
| `make migrate-tenants` | Move each user's tenant from their token's `app_metadata.tenant_id` into grantr: a new tenant's earliest user becomes its owner, and the rest its admins; a tenant grantr holds gains its claimed users who aren't members, as admins. It prints who got what, and can run again |

People are added, given roles and issued keys through grantr's API at `/grantr/v1`.
{%- if use_portal %}

## The portal

[portalr](https://lattice.alexnodeland.com/portalr/) ([`portal.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/portal.py)) is the app and admin UIs and the router of reads behind them, on the same grantr. Its settings are `PORTAL_*` in `.env`: the Supabase project the UIs sign in to, the tools the admin reads (Tempo, Loki, Prometheus through prom-label-proxy, Langfuse, LiteLLM, Grafana), and where the built UIs are.

- **The UIs aren't in a repository install of portalr.** The image builds them from the commit `pyproject.toml` pins (`PORTAL_UI_DIR=/ui`). To run them with `make serve`, `make portal-ui` builds them into `.portal-ui` (it needs git and Bun) and `PORTAL_UI_DIR=.portal-ui` serves them.
- **The first person in** is made with `make first-run EMAIL=you@example.com`: their account, if they have none, with a password it asks for; the deployment's operator, if it has none; and a tenant they own, `home`, with a workspace, `main` (`TENANT=` and `WORKSPACE=` choose others). Then they sign in at <http://localhost:{{ app_port }}/portalr/app/> and start a thread. An owner invites people from the admin.
- **It signs in on this application's own origin** (`PORTAL_SUPABASE_URL=/`): the application passes Supabase Auth's three sign-in calls (a token, the user, signing out) on to `AUTH_ADMIN_URL` or `AUTH_ISSUER`, and nothing else, so it works with local Supabase or stackr's GoTrue alone, on this machine or a tailnet.
- **On your tailnet:** `make tailnet` serves the application on this machine's tailnet name, over HTTPS, for your tailnet only (`tailscale serve`, never `funnel`), and prints its address; `make tailnet-off` stops it. It uses one port, `TAILNET_PORT` (443), refuses one something else is served on, and changes nothing in `.env`. It needs Tailscale on this machine and the device, and installs nothing.
- **A tool without an address, or whose project isn't named, is left out**: its card says so. Spend is read with a key of its own, `PORTAL_LITELLM_KEY`, from `make portal-key` in stackr: it reads teams' spend and nothing else, and the gateway's master key never reaches the application.
{%- if hosting == 'host' %}
- **On a single host the portal is required:** the application doesn't start without `PORTAL_SUPABASE_KEY` in `.env`, or with a project that isn't at an https address. The overlay sets the project to `https://app.<STACKR_DOMAIN>`, this application's own address, where stackr's edge serves the sign-in calls, so a browser stays on one origin. Invitations are off ([the template's guide](https://lattice.alexnodeland.com/stackr/guides/template/)).
{%- endif %}
- **Its audit records** (`portalr.access` and `grantr.audit`) reach Loki through the Collector: `LOG_CONFIG` puts both loggers at INFO in every application this template generates, whether or not it has the portal.
{%- endif %}

{% endif -%}
