## Native files

Files in an app's own format, a Word document or a workbook, are kept as artifacts, read and edited by the app's pinned CLI ([`files.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/files.py), [native files](https://lattice.alexnodeland.com/artifactr/files/)). No app runs until `FILES_CRAFTS` names it, in `.env` or for one run:

```bash
make up FILES="wordcraft gridcraft pdfcraft"
```

- **Each app runs in a worker of its own,** `files-<app>`, built from its release as `scripts/crafts/` pins it, checked against its SHA-256 before it is unpacked. A worker is read-only, isn't root, and is alone on a network of its own, `files-<app>`, which has no route out; only this application reaches it, with the worker's own token, `FILES_<APP>_TOKEN`, which `make env` generates. Nothing a call starts outlives it. Native files need `make up` (Compose 2.20 or later): `make serve` can't reach the workers.
- **Each app is switched per workspace,** as `files:<app>`, installed only while it runs: WordCraft, once `FILES_CRAFTS` names it, is on in every workspace of every tenant until an admin switches it off; the others are off until an admin switches them on.
{%- if use_portal %}
- **People edit in the app's browser build,** on an origin of its own: GridCraft's, PdfCraft's, CADCraft's and WordCraft's are served as released on this machine's loopback, ports {{ app_port + 2 }}, {{ app_port + 3 }}, {{ app_port + 4 }} and {{ app_port + 5 }}, with lattice's shim, which only `PORTAL_URL` may frame. Elsewhere, such as on a tailnet, serve each on a port of its own and name it in `FILES_EDITORS` ([the template's guide](https://lattice.alexnodeland.com/stackr/guides/template/#native-files)).
{%- endif %}
- **Files arrive through `NativeFiles.upload`, in code:** nothing in lattice makes one from an upload over HTTP yet. People check a file out and back in from the portal.

{% endif -%}
## How it is wired

It talks to stackr's ports only, through the settings in `.env` ([`settings.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/settings.py)). Pointing them at other services (hosted Supabase, another OTLP backend, another OpenAI-compatible gateway) changes settings, never code.

| Port | Settings | In the code |
|---|---|---|
| Telemetry | `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` (OTLP over HTTP), `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME` | [`telemetry.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/telemetry.py): the libraries' `configure_telemetry`, on when the endpoint is set, tracing requests, queries and model calls. Traces reach Langfuse through stackr's Collector, so the application sends it only scores |
| Database | `DATABASE_URL`, `DATABASE_SCHEMA`, `DATABASE_ADMIN_URL` | [`database.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/database.py): the libraries' SQL storage, migrated at startup, in a schema of its own outside the `public` schema Supabase's API serves. With `DATABASE_ADMIN_URL`, the tables' owner, PostgreSQL keeps tenants apart itself: the application works as `DATABASE_URL`'s role, which owns nothing{% if access == 'grantr' %} and which `make db-roles` creates first{% endif %}, and migrates as the owner, granting that role the tables |
{%- if access == 'grantr' %}
| Identity | `AUTH_JWKS_URL`, `AUTH_JWT_SECRET`, `AUTH_AUDIENCE`, `AUTH_ISSUER`, `AUTH_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` | [`auth.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/auth.py): Supabase's ES256 tokens against its published keys, or HS256 tokens with a legacy secret; [`access.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/access.py): the tenant, and what grantr allows |
{%- else %}
| Identity | `AUTH_JWKS_URL`, `AUTH_JWT_SECRET`, `AUTH_AUDIENCE`, `AUTH_ISSUER`, `AUTH_TENANT_CLAIM` | [`auth.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/auth.py): Supabase's ES256 tokens against its published keys, or HS256 tokens with a legacy secret |
{%- endif %}
| LLM gateway | `LITELLM_BASE_URL`, `LITELLM_API_KEY`, `LITELLM_TENANT_KEYS`, `LITELLM_MODEL`, `LITELLM_GUARDRAILS` | [`gateway.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/gateway.py): agents on the `default` model group, each request with its tenant's key and the `pii-mask` and `prompt-injection` guardrails |
{%- if use_tracker %}
| Task tracker | `TRACKER_CREDENTIALS`, `TRACKER_BINDINGS`, `TRACKER_BUDGET` | [`tracking.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/tracking.py): relayr's connector to {% if tracker == 'plane' %}Plane{% else %}Vikunja{% endif %}, where each tenant has a workspace and a user of its own. The example files each rule whose runs are dead-lettered as one item, and comments on it for each; a follower per bound workspace publishes what people change in its project as `tracker:` events. stackr's [tracker guide](https://lattice.alexnodeland.com/stackr/guides/trackers/) provisions a tenant, and its `make tracker-check` checks one |
{%- endif %}
| Evaluation data | `LANGFUSE_BASE_URL`, `LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY`, `LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY` | [`scores.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/scores.py): each workspace's feedback mirrored to Langfuse scores on the traces it is about, a score per field by evalr's score mapping, carrying on after a cursor saved in the workspace when the application restarts |

The app profile ([`compose.yaml`](compose.yaml)) joins the stack's networks and reaches its services by name, at the addresses in [`stackr.env`](stackr.env); `STACK_DATABASE_URL` points it at another database there, and `STACK_DATABASE_ADMIN_URL` names that database's owner, for row-level security.
{%- if use_reflexr %}

When the application stops, reflexr's reactor starts no new runs and gives the running ones `STOP_GRACE` to end ([`automation.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/automation.py)), within the app profile's `stop_grace_period`. A run still going then is recorded as an abandoned attempt, and the next start retries it after its rule's backoff.
{%- endif %}
{%- if use_artifactr %}

artifactr's runner stops the agent's turns still going when the application stops, and each records that it stopped ([`collaboration.py`](src/{{ package_name }}/collaboration.py)).
{%- endif %}
